<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicaea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as İznik, Iznik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicaea, modern İznik in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, was an ancient and Byzantine city on the eastern shore of Lake Ascania. It hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> in 325, which produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>, and the Second Council in 787. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> held it as their capital from about 1081 until the First Crusade restored it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1097; from 1204 to 1261 it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1331.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicaea.jpg" alt="Nicaea" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iznik_Roman_Theatre_1645.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nicaea lay in the northwestern Anatolian region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>, in a fertile basin at the eastern end of Lake Ascanius, between ranges of hills. Its west wall rose from the lake itself, which protected it from siege on that side and kept a source of supplies open. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes a Hellenistic city laid out as a square of about 700 metres on a side, with four gates and streets meeting at right angles. After an earthquake the Emperor Hadrian visited in 123 CE and began a rebuilding, and the city was enclosed by a wall some 5 kilometres long with more than a hundred towers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a> refounded the city about 315 BCE as Antigoneia, and after his defeat at Ipsus in 301 BCE Lysimachus renamed it Nicaea for his wife. Before 280 BCE it passed to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> and became a royal seat, beginning a rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> that lasted for centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> took it in 72 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> sacked it in 258 CE. In 325 CE the first Ecumenical Council met there and adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>. The council of 787, attended by Pope Hadrian I, dealt with the iconoclastic controversy and recognised the veneration of images as legitimate.</p><p>In 1081 the city fell to Turkish allies of the rebel Nikephoros Melissenos, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> made it their capital until 1097, when it returned to Byzantine control after a month's siege with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, Nicaea escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> occupation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore Laskaris</a> was crowned emperor there in 1208 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a>, and the exiled Patriarchate of Constantinople also resided there until 1261. It surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after a long siege on 2 March 1331, and the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> became a mosque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ancient city lies within the Turkish city of İznik, whose name comes from Nicaea. Much of the wall survives, though roads have pierced it in many places, and it is a tourist destination. Excavations are under way at the Ottoman kilns where the historic tiles were made, and the ruins of a fourth-century basilica of Saint Neophytos were found under the shallow water of Lake İznik. The Church of the Dormition, one of the most important Byzantine churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, was destroyed in 1922.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicaea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Palaiologan restoration of 1261, achieved by Michael VIII from the successor state of <b>Nicaea</b>, recovered Constantinople but not the empire's substance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Until the late thirteenth century the rulers of Trebizond claimed the title of Emperor of the Romans, which put them in competition with <b>Nicaea</b> and later with the restored Byzantine Empire in Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A truce with <b>Nicaea</b> followed in 1207, a further Latin victory at the Rhyndakos river in October 1211, and the Treaty of Nymphaeum in 1214.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory at Adrianople in 1205 helped prevent the Latin Empire from consolidating its hold on the Balkans, and Ivan Asen II's alliance with <b>Nicaea</b> strengthened the Greek state that recovered Constantinople in 1261.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Suleiman exploited this disorder to win control of much of western and central Anatolia and established himself at <b>Nicaea</b>, only a short distance from Constantinople, between about 1075 and 1081; the traditional starting date of 1077 is approximate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Christians believe in one God, revealed as three persons, the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit: the doctrine of the Trinity, defined at the councils of <b>Nicaea</b> in 325 and Constantinople in 381.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
